### 3.1 The worker threads: slower with any number of workers; the cause measured, the granularity derived

A correction first. The earlier text of this item, of the design-flaws report §2.2 and of
core-forced-by-paper item (2) measured four workers slower than one and proposed removal. Avik,
2026-11-21: "You measured something slower on one workload and proposed deletion? Instead of asking
about cache layout, contention? The things that cause slowness with scaling?" That was a judgement
in place of a diagnosis. The diagnosis follows; the design question it leaves is his.

What it is, plainly: the core C can run the rewriting on several operating-system threads. Each
thread owns a double-ended queue of active pairs (src/net.c lines 192 to 201, "per-worker deques …
Chase and Lev"; `top` and `bottom` padded onto separate cache lines), pushes and pops its own at the
bottom (net_push 237 to 258, pop_own 260 to 279), and steals the oldest from another's top (steal
284 to 292), but only from a queue holding 32 or more (STEAL_AT, 282) unless it has been idle 1024
iterations (LONE_AT, 283), when it takes anything. An idle thread pauses 128 times, then calls
sched_yield up to 1024 times, then sleeps 20 to 200 µs (idle_wait, 3461 to 3474). The main thread
rewrites too, and an observation wakes every worker when its own queue holds 8 pairs and parks them
all before returning (net_observe, 3523 to 3551, WAKE_AT). The count comes from `CUBICAL_WORKERS`
(src/lang.c 5156 to 5160); the default is one thread. Every store to a wire or slot branches on a
global `net_solo` flag (net.c 15, 52 to 53, 171 to 180).

**The curve.** Brunerie whole runs, 5 interleaved rounds under `perf stat`, 4 cores, no other
process (scaling.log sha256 a9247483…, the machine ed3c3668… at main 172052738; "workers" are
threads beside the main thread):

| workers | elapsed (se) | processor time | context switches | migrations | futex calls | sched_yield calls | sleeps |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 2.78 s (0.10) | 2.77 s | 9 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 1 | 5.88 s (0.11) | 8.56 s | 7,200 | 6 | 31,300 | 3,145,000 | 960 |
| 2 | 6.71 s (0.16) | 12.78 s | 25,800 | 18 | 82,900 | 2,461,000 | 1,010 |
| 3 | 7.69 s (0.12) | 17.87 s | 51,800 | 49 | 132,000 | 2,941,000 | 1,220 |
| 4 | 7.99 s (0.14) | 18.49 s | 388,000 | 4,040 | 150,000 | 2,625,000 | 1,210 |

The library, once each: 0 workers 47.3 s (45.3 s of processor time); 3 workers 492 s (728 s;
1.08 M context switches, 1.44 M futex calls, 31.6 M sched_yield calls, 21,700 sleeps); 4 workers
547 s (784 s; 6.2 M context switches, 61,500 migrations, 1.70 M futex, 32.0 M sched_yield).

What the curve says. The loss is there with one worker, 2.1 times, and grows slowly after; three
workers beside the main thread on four cores, no oversubscription, are as slow as four. So it is not
contention that grows with the number of cores, and not oversubscription, which only adds the
388,000 context switches and 4,000 migrations at four workers and about 10% of time. The cause is
what one extra thread does to one computation.

**Where the threads spend their time** (perf, cpu clock, 2 kHz, call graphs; after-scaling.log
ac5dd5db…). With one worker the main thread has 67% of the samples and the worker 33%; with four,
the main thread 42% and each worker 14 to 16%. The main thread beside one worker spends 36% of its
own time in `pop` (its queue empty, scanning the worker's), 15% in net_observe's loop, 8% in
net_push, 7.5% in cell, 7% in flush_frees; on one thread, push and pop are a ring's index
arithmetic and do not appear. The worker's samples are kernel scheduling, sched_yield and the
`worker` loop (the steal scan inlined), with little rewriting. With four workers, 51% of all
samples are in `pop`: four threads scanning one another's queues.

**The width of the work.** A measuring copy of the machine (the repository's src with two lines
added in the single-thread ring_pop calling an instrument that histograms the ring's length at
every pop; built in the scratchpad, never committed; frontier-build.sh 5ad49046…, the copy's diff
is the two lines, binary ef37a011…) run on one thread:

| pending pairs at a pop | Brunerie (9.25 M pops) | the library (258 M pops) |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 41.7% | 46.9% |
| 2 | 20.5% | 19.5% |
| 3 to 4 | 22.8% | 23.7% |
| 5 to 8 | 9.4% | 5.2% |
| 9 to 32 | 4.5% | 2.4% |
| 33 or more | 1.0% | 2.5% |
| mean; largest | 3.4; 178 | 8.1; 1,849 |

At the granularity this executor parallelises, one active pair at a time, the workload is a chain:
85% of the time there are at most four pairs to do, and a step is about 87 ns (two-evaluators
§5.2). A queue reaches STEAL_AT in 1 to 2.5% of pops.

**The mechanisms,** as the code and the counts show them; what is read is said to be read, what is
inferred is marked:

1. No width to distribute (read: the frontier counts). Any executor that hands out single pairs
   pays a synchronisation per step of the order of the step itself.
2. The chain handed between cores (inferred from LONE_AT, pop_own and the per-thread split). With
   one or two pairs pending, the idle thread takes the single next pair once it has been idle 1024
   iterations, about a millisecond, continues the chain from its own deque, and the former owner is
   now the idle one, polling; the chain's cells move with it. The code's own comment (196 to 197)
   names the cache effect of consecutive pairs on two cores. The number of lone steals was not
   instrumented.
3. The idle thread's polling of the owner's queue lines (inferred). steal loads `top` and `bottom`
   of every other queue on every attempt; the owner stores `bottom` at every push and pop; each
   thief load pulls the line out of the owner's cache and the owner's next store misses. The padding
   makes this true sharing, not false sharing. The main thread's 8% in net_push and 36% in pop are
   consistent with it. This machine exposes no hardware counters (perf: cycles and cache-misses
   "not supported", `perf c2c` "memory events not supported"), so coherence misses were not counted;
   on hardware that exposes them, `perf c2c` on the one-worker run is the measurement.
4. The yield storm (read: the counts). 3.1 M sched_yield system calls per Brunerie run, 32 M per
   library run, from idle_wait's middle phase; 1.1 s of system time with one worker, 19 to 25 s on
   the library; with five threads on four cores, 388,000 context switches.
5. Wake and park per observation (read: the code and the futex counts; the number of observations
   not instrumented). An observation wakes every worker at 8 pending pairs and parks them all
   before returning, waiting for each to notice `running` cleared, which a worker checks only
   between pops, after an idle_wait that may be a 200 µs sleep. 31,300 futex calls per Brunerie
   run with one worker, about 15,000 wake-and-park cycles; 1.44 to 1.70 M on the library, whose
   readback is one observation per node (the comment at 3521 to 3524). This is the library's
   eleven times: a wake and a park per node read back.
6. The threaded path on the owner's own steps (inferred, not separated from 3 without counters):
   every link deferred to publish through realloc'd arrays (link 336 to 355, publish 357 to 362),
   an atomic store on every slot and wire, a thread-local lookup per push.

**The answer, derived** (Avik, 2026-11-21: "There is always a pure math answer as to the right way.
I'm not sure what you're asking." The earlier text here posed a question; it had an answer).

The steps of a run form a directed acyclic graph: a step depends on the steps that made its two
cells. Let W be the number of steps and D the length of the longest chain of dependent steps. On p
processors no executor finishes before max(W/p, D) steps (Brent's bound), whatever its scheduling,
and the speedup available is W/D. Both are measured by the same measuring copy (depth-build.sh
67c99af8…, depth.log 060b57a9…, binary 2880ba17…): every cell carries the time of the step that
made it, one more than the later of its pair's two times (a copy out of a box inherits the box
content's time if later); two clocks, one restarted at each observation (depth within an
observation) and one not (the whole run's causal depth).

| one thread | Brunerie | the library |
|---|---|---|
| steps W | 9,248,545 | 257,909,176 |
| observations | 8,230 | 96,017 |
| mean steps per observation; mean depth per observation | 1,124; 248 | 2,686; 690 |
| sum over observations of their depths, ΣD | 2,043,141 | 66,253,744 |
| W/ΣD: the speedup available with observations in sequence | 4.5 | 3.9 |
| the whole run's causal depth D | 161,807 | 8,132,174 |
| W/D: the speedup available if observations overlap wherever the net allows | 57 | 32 |
| the largest observation: W; D; W/D | 416,489; 159,639; 2.6 | 17,596,021; 4,020,209; 4.4 |

(The frontier means above, 3.4 and 8.1 pending pairs, are the same quantity seen from the ring and
agree with 4.5 and 3.9.) The distribution: on Brunerie 20% of steps lie in observations with
W/D under 3 and 66% in observations with W/D under 12; on the library 39% of steps lie in
observations with W/D under 4.

So, at the pair level with observations in sequence, which is what the executor does today, the
ceiling is 4.5 and 3.9 with infinitely many processors and no synchronisation cost; on four cores
the ceiling is under 4. The cost model fixes how much of that is reachable: a step costs s ≈ 87 ns;
a step whose cells were made on another core costs s plus the transfer of their lines, m, which is
of the order of s on this hardware (not counted here: no counters; of the order of 100 ns on
current processors); and a decision to move work costs at least one such transfer. The time on p
cores is then at least ΣD·(s + m·h) where h is the fraction of critical-path steps that cross cores.
The lone-steal rule moves the chain's next pair whenever a thread has been idle a millisecond, so on
a chain h is near 1 and the critical path costs 2s per step instead of s: the measured 2.1 times
slower with one worker is this term, before the yields and the wake-and-park. The condition for a
gain at a granularity of g dependent steps handed off at once is g·s ≫ m, and the observations'
chains are 248 and 690 steps long on average (22 and 60 µs): long enough for one hand-off each,
not for one per step. STEAL_AT = 32 is a bound of that kind (2.8 µs of work per steal); LONE_AT
overrides it; and the Brunerie computation itself, the largest observation, has W/D = 2.6: a chain.

At the level of observations the cut is zero: the nets of two observations share no cell (a shared
definition is read out of its box by copy, lazy_copy), so a partition by observation has no
cross-core edges at all, synchronises once per observation (thousands of steps), and its width is
the whole run's W/D: 57 on the Brunerie run, 32 on the library, against 4.5 and 3.9 inside
observations. That is the mathematical answer to the granularity: the independent unit of this
machine's work is the observation, not the pair, by a factor of about ten in available parallelism
and by the whole of the synchronisation cost. What realizes it is not in net.c: the host (lang.c,
syntax.c) issues one observation at a time and holds shared mutable state between them (the record
table and its interning, the arena, the store), so the 57 and 32 are the net's bound, and the host's
sequencing is what stands between the machine and them. That is a fact about where the work is, for
the sessions that own the infrastructure; the step DAG's depth was not consulted when the deques
were written, and the pure bound settles what any scheduler at the pair level could have done here.

The earlier twelve-pair series (8.53 s against 2.85 s) and this one (7.99 against 2.78, under
perf stat) agree.

### 3.2 The free-dimension cut (A8)

What it is: when a value in a box is built, the machine walks it once to record which dimensions it
mentions, and a pending substitution of other dimensions on that box is cut to that set (src/net.c
line 576 onward; its comment names its origin: PLAN.md revision 7.9, §18.16 "A8 — Proposal",
a Claude plan, not the paper). Its second part, A8b, a bound computed by walking the pending net, cost
2.4 times what it saved and is removed (lines 590 to 598 record it).

Forced: nothing; the code's own comment says answers are the same without it. Measured (a8b.log,
thirty rounds, the reading's own time): with A8 0.070 s, without 0.088 s; whole runs unchanged
(2.65 against 2.67 s Brunerie, 45.3 against 45.0 s library, twelve pairs); its walks are 4% of the
reading (box_fd, fd_cell, fd_port). So it is the one Claude addition that measurably helps a reading
and measurably changes no run. Whether a 20% gain on one reading earns 600 lines of walking in the
core is a design decision; the calculus's own answer to "a substitution of a dimension the value does
not mention" is that the held substitution is applied as the value is read and meets nothing (§6.1),
which is what happens without A8 at 0.088 s.

### 3.3 The remembered elaboration (x_checked's store entry)

What it is: the solved type and term of a declaration, kept in the store under a hash of its surface
forms, the implicit mask and the checker's identity; on a hit the elaboration's rounds are skipped
(src/syntax.c, `x_checked`, declared at line 1093, called at 1483 and 1513; the entry's code at
6200 to 6220 and 6273 to 6278 of the version read on 2026-11-17, unread since).

Forced: nothing. Paper §10.1 asks that a reuse record what was observed; this key does not record the
signatures and instances the elaboration depended on (the code's own comment), so it is a cache, sound
only because acceptance is never taken from it. Measured by removing it: every value and verdict
identical; warm loads five times slower (Brunerie 0.91 s to 2.69 s, the library 4.42 s to 23.3 s),
because without it the elaboration's rounds, each running the checker to solve holes, run on every
load. What it hides is the real number: the front end costs 2.7 s on a computation whose evaluation is
0.07 s. The fix the paper names is a certificate (§10.1, §10.5) that records what was read; the fix the
numbers name is a front end that does not take forty times the evaluation.

### 3.4 Record interning and the digest of swept records

What it is: equal compiled bodies are made one pointer (src/lang.c `rec_intern`, line 1983, called
at 2133 and 2305), and between top-level forms records nothing reaches are freed, those only a key
named keeping a SHA-256 digest of their body (`records_sweep`, line 2089, called at 3642). Both exist,
by their own comments as read on 2026-11-17, so that the memo would find equal code again.

The memo and the keys were removed by another session on 2026-11-18 (0bace3c2e; `grep` for key
functions in net.c finds none at 98f3e4647). Whether the interning and the digests still do anything
after that removal was not re-read: the functions exist (lines above); their callers' purpose now is
unread. Before the removal they cost about 3% (interning: syn_hash, syn_eq, rec_intern) and about 4%
(syn_digest, sha256_update) of the Brunerie run. If the memo was their only consumer, they are dead
weight at 7% of a run.

### 3.5 The checker's identity mixes the build time

src/lang.c lines 4923 and 5162: `__DATE__ " " __TIME__` is mixed into the store's keys, so every
build of the machine is a new checker and the whole store is re-verified (94 s cold, evaluator-speed
item (6)). Forced: §10.5 asks that the certificate be sound, which this over-satisfies; nothing forces
the build time. The change: name the checker by the hash of the machine's source bytes and the library
texts, so a rebuild of unchanged sources keeps every certificate. Avik's call, noted in
core-forced-by-paper.

